"CONSUMER CONTRACTS\nBerkson v. Gogo LLC, 97 F. Supp. 3d 359 (E.D.N.Y. 2015), might be read to state that the\nclassification of an agreement as a browsewrap agreement means that the agreement will not be\nenforced against an individual. The judge who decided Berkson later distinguished it and enforced\nthe browsewrap agreement in the case of a \"sophisticated business person.\" Salameno v. Gogo,\n2016 WL 4005783 (E.D.N.Y. 2016). In Soliman v. Subway Franchisee Advertising Fund Trust,\nLtd., the court stated that the \"[c]lassification of web-based contracts alone ... does not resolve\nthe notice inquiry.\" 999 F.3d 828, 835 (2d Cir. 2021) (quoting Meyer v. Uber Techs., Inc., 868\nF.3d 66, 76 (2d Cir. 2017)). Instead, the court stated, the analysis looks at the \"totality of the\ncircumstances.\" Id. at 831. See, e.g., Nevarez v. Forty Niners Football Co., 2017 WL 3492110, at\n*8 (N.D. Cal. Aug. 15, 2017) (evaluating the disclosure of terms in the totality of the\ncircumstances, and holding that, \"[i]n any event, regardless of whether the Ticketmaster Website's\n[terms of use (TOU)] are most appropriately labeled as a 'browsewrap' or a 'clickwrap' agreement,\nthe Court finds that Plaintiffs assented to the Ticketmaster Website's TOU in purchasing their\ntickets and parking passes on the Ticketmaster Website\"). Similarly, in Wollen v. Gulf Stream\nRestoration & Cleaning, LLC, 259 A.3d 867, 875 (N.J. Super. Ct. App. Div. July 9, 2021), the\ncourt, noting Berkson's characterization of various types of online agreements, stated, \"Regardless\nof a web-based agreement's characterization, however, the pertinent inquiry is whether the user\nwas provided with reasonable notice of the applicable terms, based on the design and layout of the\nwebsite.\" See Nancy S. Kim et al., Notice and Assent Through Technological Change: The\nEnduring Relevance of the Work of the ABA Joint Working Group on Electronic Contracting\nPractices, 75 BUS. LAW. 1725, 1744-1745 (2020) (\"[T]he four-part test in the Browse-Wrap\nArticle can be used in any online contract formation presentation-independent of how the\npresentation is categorized-to analyze the validity of assent. The test applies equally well to\nbrowse-wrap, scroll-wrap, sign-in-wrap, and other presentations on smartphones and other\ndevices, not just the browse-wrap presentations seen by the Working Group in 2003.\").\nFocusing on the state supreme-court and appellate-court decisions through 2022 further\nsupports the notice-and-opportunity-to-review doctrine. Those cases include a decision by the\nSupreme Court of Arkansas (Roller v. TV Guide Online Holdings, LLC, 2013 Ark. 285 (Ark.\n2013)) and the appellate courts of California (Long v. Provide Commerce, Inc. 200 Cal. Rptr. 3d\n117 (Cal. Ct. App. 2016)); Florida (Vitacost.com, Inc. v. McCants, 210 So. 3d 761 (Fla. Dist. Ct.\nApp. 2017), and MetroPCS Commc'ns, Inc. v. Porter, 273 So. 3d 1025 (Fla. Dist. Ct. App. 2018));\nGeorgia (Thornton v. Uber, 858 S.E.2d 255 (Ga. Ct. App. 2021), reconsideration denied (June 16,\n2021), cert. denied (Oct. 5, 2021)); Missouri (Major v. McCallister, 302 S.W.3d 227 (Mo. Ct. App.\n2009)); and New Jersey (Hoffman v. Supplements Togo Mgmt., LLC, 18 A.3d 210 (N.J. Super.\nCt. App. Div. 2011)). The circuit-court opinions evaluating the enforceability of browsewraps\ninclude one by the U.S. Court of Appeals for the First Circuit applying Massachusetts law; three\nby the U.S. Court of Appeals for the Second Circuit, applying Washington, New York, and\nCalifornia law, respectively; one by the U.S. Court of Appeals for the Third Circuit applying New\nJersey law; one by the U.S. Courts of Appeals for the Fifth Circuit applying Illinois law; one by\n65\n@§2\n"